    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d513">

                <pb facs="0544" n="426" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg426"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">612.0023/93</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Mexican Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Téllez</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-05-29">May 29, 1929</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of Your Excellency’s note No. 2941 of May 13, 1929,<anchor n="84"
                        xml:id="d513-f1"/> stating that the port of Yavaros, State of Sonora, has
                    been reopened to international traffic, and that, with the opening of the
                    Customhouse at that place, none of the border or seacoast Customhouses, which
                    were closed by reason of the recent insurrection, now remains closed.</p>
                <p>In this connection the Department begs to refer to its note of May 14, 1929,
                    touching this question.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed>J. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Reuben Clark, Jr.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
